<robot name="mecanum_wheel" xmlns:xacro="http://ros.org/wiki/xacro" xmlns:gz="http://gazebosim.org/schema">
  <xacro:macro name="mecanum_wheel"
               params="name
                       prefix
                       parent_frame
                       mass
                       size
                       velocity_limit:=100
                       effort_limit:=100000
                       mu_forward:=1.0
                       mu_lateral:=0.05
                       slip_forward:=0.05
                       slip_lateral:=0.35
                       mesh_visual:=''
                       mesh_visual_has_color:=False
                       scale:=1.0
                       color:='0 0 0 1'
                       joint_parent_fr_wheel_fr
                       base_root_fr
                       fdir1_base_root_fr">
    <!--
    ============================================================================================
    Explanation of default joint limits, effort_limit and velocity_limit.
    ============================================================================================
    The 'effort' and 'velocity' limits for this joint are intentionally set to extremely high, non-physical values.
    This is a common and deliberate practice in simulation for a specific reason: to model an "ideal" actuator.

    Why these default values (effort_limit:=100000 velocity_limit:=100)?
    By setting virtually infinite limits, we ensure that the simulator's physics engine will never be the limiting
    factor in the joint's performance. This allows developers to test high-level control algorithms (e.g., navigation,
    trajectory following) without being constrained by the physical limitations of real-world motors.
    In essence, we are testing the software logic in isolation.

    Context for the default values:
    effort_limit = "100000" (Unit: Newton-meters, N·m).
    This represents the maximum torque the joint can apply. To put this into perspective, a high-performance
    passenger car engine produces between 200-500 N·m of torque. A value of 100,000 N·m is more in the realm of
    industrial cranes or ship engines. It is an astronomically high value for a robot wheel.

    - velocity_limit = "100" (Unit: radians/second).
    This translates to approximately 955 RPM (Revolutions Per Minute). For context, a typical educational robot
    wheel (like a TurtleBot) spins at around 70-100 RPM. So, 955 RPM is extremely fast.

    If you need a realistic simulation:
    If your goal is to simulate dynamics that are faithful to a physical robot, you should replace these values with
    the specifications from your actuator's datasheet. For a typical medium-sized mobile robot, realistic values would
    be in the range of:
    - Velocity: 10 to 30 rad/s
    - Effort:   10 to 100 N·m
    -->

    <xacro:include filename="$(find eut_robotics_description)/urdf/common/all_commons.xacro"/>

    <!--
    A wheel is a cylinder with its axis along the y-axis, which means that:
    size_x = 2*radius, size_y = thickness, size_z = 2*radius
    -->

    <xacro:link_c
      name="${name}"
      prefix="${prefix}"
      use_visual="True"
      use_collision="True"
      use_inertia="True"
      mass="${mass}"
      axis="y"
      size="${size}"
      mesh_visual="${mesh_visual}"
      mesh_visual_has_color="${mesh_visual_has_color}"
      mesh_collision=""
      scale="${scale}"
      color="${color}"
      vci_pose="0 0 0 0 0 0"/>

    <xacro:property name="j_wheel" value="${joint_parent_fr_wheel_fr.split()}"/>

    <joint name="${prefix}${name}_joint" type="continuous">
      <parent link="${parent_frame}"/>
      <child link="${prefix}${name}_link"/>
      <origin rpy="${j_wheel[3]} ${j_wheel[4]} ${j_wheel[5]}" xyz="${j_wheel[0]} ${j_wheel[1]} ${j_wheel[2]}"/>
      <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
      <limit effort="${effort_limit}" velocity="${velocity_limit}"/>
    </joint>

    <gazebo reference="${prefix}${name}_link">
      <max_contacts>2</max_contacts> <!-- Default is 10 -->
      <collision>
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <!--
              Read the explanation at the end of this file about how to set fdir1 for mecanum wheels.
              -->
              <fdir1 gz:expressed_in="${base_root_fr}">${fdir1_base_root_fr}</fdir1>
              <!--
              Reference 'http://sdformat.org/tutorials?tut=sdformat_urdf_extensions&cat=specification#gazebo-elements-for-links'
              Friction coefficient, in the direction of the wheel rolling, along the contact surface.
              This coefficient should be high enough to allow the wheel to roll without slipping,
              from 0.8 a 1.5.
              High traction along forward direction
              -->
              <mu>${mu_forward}</mu>
              <!--
              Friction coefficient, in axial direction (the direction of the wheel axis), along the
              contact surface.
              This 'can be somehow assimilated to the direcion in which the rollers are moving'.
              This coefficient should be low enough to allow the rollers to move freely, from 0.03 to
              0.1.
              Low friction laterally to emulate rollers.
              -->
              <mu2>${mu_lateral}</mu2>
              <slip1>${slip_forward}</slip1>
              <slip2>${slip_lateral}</slip2>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
    </gazebo>
  </xacro:macro>
</robot>

<!--
====================================
Explanation about the mecanum wheels
====================================

Geometric and contact context
=============================

* The wheel’s joint uses 'axis = (0, 1, 0)' -> the wheel rotates about y-axis.
* The ground is horizontal -> the contact normal is Z; therefore the contact plane (tangent) is X–Y in the wheel’s local
frame.
* In ODE/Gazebo, 'fdir1' must lie in the contact plane (tangent) -> its Z component is 0.

Conventional vs. Mecanum (the "cylinder + anisotropy" model)
============================================================

* If you set 'fdir1 = (1, 0, 0)', 'mu = 1.0', 'mu2 = 0.05', you model a conventional wheel: "high" friction aligned with
+X (forward rolling), "low" in the orthogonal direction (Y). You get correct forward/reverse, but no pure lateral
translation.

* For a mecanum wheel, with this model you must align the high friction direction with the direction perpendicular to
the roller’s axis in the X–Y plane:

  * The roller’s axis must have low friction (it "freewheels" like a roller) -> that corresponds to 'mu2' (not to
    'fdir1').
  * Therefore, 'fdir1' is perpendicular to the roller’s axis (in X–Y).
    If the roller’s axis projected onto X–Y is a=(a_x, a_y, 0), take:

    fdir1 = R_{+90} a = (-a_y, a_x, 0)

    and normalize if necessary.

Observation: for friction, a line has no orientation; 'v' and '−v' are equivalent**. Pick one sign and be consistent across wheels.
============

"X" layout (rollers for the four wheels "pointing in an X shape" when viewed from above)
========================================================================================

In the standard "X" configuration (REP-103, wheel rotates about Y, contact plane X–Y), the 'fdir1' vectors to set per
wheel are:

* Front-Left (FL) and Rear-Right (RR) -> +45 deg, from +X toward +Y

  * Symbolic: 'fdir1 = (cos 45°,  sin 45°, 0)'
  * Numeric:  'fdir1 = (0.70710678, 0.70710678, 0)'

* Front-Right (FR) and Rear-Left (RL) -> −45 deg, from +X toward −Y

  * Symbolic: 'fdir1 = (cos 45°,  sin −45°, 0)'
  * Numeric:  'fdir1 = (0.70710678, −0.70710678, 0)'

With this, a Twist with 'vy != 0' (and 'vx = wz = 0') yields pure lateral translation.

Recommended friction parameters
===============================

* 'mu = 1.0'  -> high friction along 'fdir1' (perpendicular to the roller’s axis).
* 'mu2 = 0.05' -> low friction in the orthogonal in-plane direction (approx. the "roller axis").
* 'slip1', 'slip2' as per your tuning.

Operational verification
========================

1. 'vx != 0, vy = 0, wz = 0' -> correct forward/reverse.
2. 'vx = 0, vy != 0, wz = 0' -> pure lateral (sign according to your convention).
3. If the lateral sign does not match your convention, invert the Y component of all four 'fdir1' vectors
(or review the FL/FR/RL/RR mapping).
-->
